{"experiments":[
  {
    "hypothesis":"For the objective of increasing drug-likeness (task_id=103) in successful edits, molecules that retain their Murcko scaffold achieve a higher average target_delta than those that change their scaffold.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare mean target_delta between scaffold‐retained vs scaffold‐changed edits for task_id=103 in train.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter rows where task_id == 103 and target_delta is not null.\n2. Split the filtered data into two groups based on murcko_scaffold_retained (True vs False).\n3. Compute group means and standard deviations of target_delta.\n4. Conduct an independent two‐sample t-test (or Welch’s t-test if variances differ) to assess whether the mean target_delta differs between the two groups.\n5. Generate a bar chart with error bars showing mean ± standard error for each group.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of group means, standard deviations, and sample sizes. 2. t-test statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value. 3. Bar chart comparing mean target_delta for scaffold‐retained vs scaffold‐changed."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"In successful permeability optimization edits (task_id=105), insert operations produce larger increases in molecular weight (MW_delta) but also incur higher synthetic accessibility penalties (SA_delta) compared to delete operations.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Quantify and compare MW_delta and SA_delta for insert vs delete actions in task_id=105 successful edits.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter for task_id == 105, drop rows with null MW_delta or SA_delta.\n2. Filter rows where action_type is either 'insert' or 'delete'.\n3. For each action_type group, calculate mean and standard deviation of MW_delta and SA_delta.\n4. Perform two multivariate comparisons: (a) MW_delta by action_type via t-test; (b) SA_delta by action_type via t-test.\n5. Create side‐by‐side boxplots: one for MW_delta and one for SA_delta across action types.",
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics table for MW_delta and SA_delta by action_type. 2. t-test results for both comparisons (statistics and p-values). 3. Two boxplots illustrating the distributions."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Unsuccessful edits exhibit greater diversity of structural edits per prompt than successful edits, as measured by the number of unique edit strings for each prompt.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare edit‐string diversity (unique count per prompt) between train_parquet and train_neg_parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null 'edit'.\n2. For each dataset, group by prompt and count unique values of the 'edit' column. Record counts per prompt.\n3. For each dataset, compute the distribution (mean, median, IQR) of unique‐edit counts across prompts.\n4. Use a Mann–Whitney U test to compare prompt‐level unique‐edit count distributions between the two datasets.\n5. Plot side-by-side violin plots of unique‐edit counts for successful vs unsuccessful datasets.",
      "deliverables":"1. Distribution summary table of unique edit counts per prompt for both datasets. 2. Mann–Whitney U test statistic and p-value. 3. Violin plot comparing edit diversity."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"In water‐solubility optimization edits (task_id=101) within successful examples, edits that increase molecular weight by more than 50 Da are more likely to produce a negative change in QED (QED_delta < 0) than edits with MW_delta ≤ 50 Da.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether large MW increases (>50 Da) are associated with negative QED_delta for task_id=101 in train.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter for task_id == 101 and non‐null MW_delta and QED_delta.\n2. Create a binary feature high_MW_increase = (MW_delta > 50).\n3. For high_MW_increase True/False groups, compute counts of QED_delta < 0 and QED_delta ≥ 0.\n4. Build a 2×2 contingency table and perform chi‐squared or Fisher’s exact test to evaluate association.\n5. Display a grouped bar chart of proportions of negative‐QED outcomes by MW increase category.",
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table with counts and proportions. 2. Test statistic and p‐value. 3. Bar chart of negative‐QED proportions for each MW_delta category."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The correlation between synthetic accessibility change (SA_delta) and target property improvement (target_delta) is stronger in successful edits than in unsuccessful edits.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare the SA_delta vs target_delta correlation coefficients in train.parquet vs train_neg.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets; drop rows with null SA_delta or target_delta in each.\n2. For each dataset, compute Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients between SA_delta and target_delta.\n3. Use Fisher’s Z‐transformation to test whether the two Pearson correlation coefficients differ significantly.\n4. Create scatterplots of SA_delta vs target_delta with overlaid regression lines for successful and unsuccessful datasets side by side.\n5. Annotate each plot with its correlation coefficient and p-value.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of Pearson and Spearman correlations and sample sizes for each dataset. 2. Z‐test statistic and p-value comparing correlations. 3. Paired scatterplots with regression lines."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Within unsuccessful edits, the probability of retaining the Murcko scaffold differs significantly across the three action types (insert, replace, delete).",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether murcko_scaffold_retained rates vary by action_type in train_neg.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null murcko_scaffold_retained or action_type.\n2. Group by action_type and compute counts of scaffold retained (True) vs changed (False).\n3. Assemble a 3×2 contingency table (action_type × scaffold retention).\n4. Perform a chi‐squared test for independence to assess whether retention rates differ by action_type.\n5. Visualize retention proportions per action_type with a stacked bar chart.",
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table with counts and proportions. 2. Chi-squared statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value. 3. Stacked bar chart of scaffold retention rates by action type."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Prompts containing the keyword 'polar' or 'hydrophilic' are associated with larger target_delta improvements in water‐solubility tasks (task_id=101) than prompts without those keywords.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess whether certain textual cues in prompts correlate with greater target property gains for water solubility edits.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter for task_id == 101 and non‐null target_delta.\n2. Create a boolean column keyword_prompt = True if prompt (lowercase) contains 'polar' or 'hydrophilic', else False.\n3. Split data into keyword_prompt True vs False groups, compute mean and median target_delta for each.\n4. Apply a Mann–Whitney U test to check for differences in target_delta distributions.\n5. Plot boxplots of target_delta for keyword vs non‐keyword prompts.",
      "deliverables":"1. Summary stats (mean, median, IQR) of target_delta by keyword_prompt. 2. Mann–Whitney U test statistic and p-value. 3. Boxplot comparing target_delta distributions."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, replace operations yield a narrower molecular weight change distribution (lower variance in MW_delta) than insert or delete operations.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare the variance of MW_delta across action types in train.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; drop rows with null MW_delta or action_type.\n2. Group data by action_type and compute sample variance of MW_delta for each group.\n3. Use Levene’s test to assess whether variances differ significantly across the three groups.\n4. Display a table of variances and test results, and plot density estimates of MW_delta for each action_type overlaid in one figure.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of MW_delta variances and sample sizes by action_type. 2. Levene’s test statistic and p-value. 3. Overlaid density plot of MW_delta for insert, replace, and delete."
    }
  }
]}
